1:18:211 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 21 Sekundengovernment established the first schools for the deaf in Nicaraguan history.
1:18:271 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 27 SekundenBefore that, deaf Nicaraguans were largely isolated from each other, each developing their own home sign systems
1:18:351 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 35 Sekundenwithin their families, much like the children Golden Meadow studied. But when these children were brought together in
1:18:431 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 43 Sekundenschools, something extraordinary happened.
1:18:471 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 47 SekundenThe first cohort of students arrived with their individual home sign systems and began communicating with each other.
1:18:551 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 55 SekundenTheir teachers were trying to teach them to lipread Spanish, which mostly didn't work. But the children were doing something far more interesting on the
1:19:041 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 4 Sekundenplayground and in the hallways. They were pooling their home sign systems, negotiating shared meanings and
1:19:121 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 12 Sekundengradually developing a common communication system. This first generation system which linguists call
1:19:201 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 20 Sekundenlinguos nicaragu language with limited grammatical
1:19:281 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 28 Sekundenstructure. But here's where it gets truly wild.
1:19:331 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 33 SekundenWhen the next cohort of younger children arrived at the school a few years later, they were exposed to this pigeon from an
1:19:401 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 40 Sekundenearly age. And what they did with it was transform it. They didn't just learn it,
1:19:471 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 47 Sekundenthey complexified it. They added grammatical markers. They developed consistent rules for verb agreement, for
1:19:551 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 55 Sekundenspatial reference, for embedding one clause inside another. They turned a pigeon into a creole, a full rich
1:20:051 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 5 Sekundennatural language now known as idiom signos Nicaragu or Nicaraguan sign language. And they did this
1:20:131 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 13 Sekundenspontaneously as children without anyone teaching them to do it. Judy Keull and
1:20:201 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 20 SekundenSangos and other linguists who documented this process recognized it as something almost unprecedented.
1:20:281 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 28 Sekundenthe birth of a new language observed in real time under conditions that allowed scientific study.
1:20:351 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 35 SekundenSangas working with Golden Meadow and others was particularly interested in what the emergence of this language
1:20:421 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 42 Sekundenmeant for the cognitive abilities of its users.
1:20:461 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 46 SekundenAnd she found that the second generation signers, the ones who had access to the full grammatical language, outperformed
1:20:541 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 54 Sekundenthe first generation signers on certain cognitive tasks, specifically tasks involving spatial reasoning and theory
1:21:011 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 1 Sekundeof mind, same community, same schools, same cultural background. The primary
1:21:101 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 10 Sekundendifference was the richness of the linguistic system they'd been exposed to during childhood. And that difference in linguistic experience produced
1:21:181 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 18 Sekundenmeasurable differences in how they thought. The richer language didn't just let them communicate more effectively.
1:21:261 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 26 SekundenIt equipped them with cognitive tools that their predecessors with the more limited pigeon didn't fully have. Let
1:21:341 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 34 Sekundenthat settle for a moment. Because this Nicaraguan story is one of the most elegant natural experiments in the
1:21:431 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 43 Sekundenhistory of cognitive science, you can't ethically deprive children of language to study the effects.
1:21:511 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 51 SekundenBut history sometimes creates conditions that approximate that experiment. And when researchers are paying attention,
1:21:581 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 58 Sekundenthe results can be extraordinarily revealing.
1:22:021 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 2 SekundenAnd what they reveal is consistent with everything else we've been circling around. Language is not the source of
1:22:091 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 9 Sekundenthought. Thought exists without it. The home signers prove that. The first generation Nicaraguan signers prove
1:22:171 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 17 Sekundenthat. But language is a powerful amplifier and organizer of thought. It provides structures that make certain
1:22:251 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 25 Sekundencognitive operations easier, faster, more precise, and more sharable.
1:22:321 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 32 SekundenWithout those structures, you can still think, but there are ceilings you hit, particularly in domains that involve
1:22:411 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 41 Sekundenabstraction, precision, or reasoning about things that aren't physically present.
1:22:481 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 48 SekundenThis is actually a good moment to think about number because number is one of the domains where the evidence for
1:22:561 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 56 Sekundenlinguistic effects on cognition is strongest and most dramatic.
1:23:021 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 2 SekundenRemember how we talked about the pira earlier? Well, actually we haven't gotten to them yet in this telling and
1:23:101 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 10 Sekundenthey're too important to skip. So let me take you there now. The Pira are a small indigenous group living along the Mai
1:23:191 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 19 SekundenRiver in the Amazon rainforest of Brazil. There are only a few hundred of them and their language, also called
1:23:271 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 27 SekundenPira, has some features that have made it one of the most debated languages in modern linguistics.
1:23:341 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 34 SekundenThe person most associated with Pira research is Daniel Everett, a linguist who first went to the Amazon in the late
1:23:431 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 43 Sekunden1970s as a missionary and ended up spending decades living with the Pira, learning their language, and eventually
1:23:521 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 52 Sekundenpublishing claims that shook the foundations of linguistic theory.
1:23:571 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 57 SekundenAmong the many unusual features Everett documented, one stands out for our purposes.
1:24:041 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 4 SekundenPira has no precise number words, none.
1:24:081 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 8 SekundenThere are approximate terms that researchers have translated roughly as small amount and larger amount, but
1:24:161 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 16 Sekundennothing that consistently means 1, 2, 3, or any specific quantity. And this isn't
1:24:231 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 23 Sekundenbecause the pira can't perceive quantities.
1:24:261 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 26 SekundenIt's that their language doesn't encode them with precision.
1:24:311 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 31 SekundenSo what happens when you test pira speakers on numerical tasks?
1:24:361 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 36 SekundenPeter Gordon, a psychologist at Colia, published a study in 2004 in which he gave Pira speakers simple matching
1:24:451 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 45 Sekundentasks. Here are some nuts. Now put out the same number of nuts from your pile.
1:24:521 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 52 SekundenFor small quantities, one or two or three, they did fine. But as the quantities grew beyond three or four, their accuracy dropped dramatically.
1:25:031 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 3 SekundenThey could approximate, but they couldn't match exactly. And the errors weren't random. They showed the
1:25:111 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 11 Sekundensignature pattern of approximate number estimation.
1:25:141 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 14 SekundenThe same system that rats and infants use, a system that gets fuzzier as quantities get larger.
1:25:221 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 22 SekundenNow, this doesn't mean the pira are less intelligent. It means they're relying on the approximate number system that all
1:25:291 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 29 Sekundenhumans share without the precision boost that number words provide.
1:25:361 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 36 SekundenAnd studies with other groups support this interpretation.
1:25:401 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 40 SekundenElizabeth Spelka and others at Harvard have shown that precise number concepts, the ability to distinguish exactly seven
1:25:491 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 49 Sekundenfrom exactly 8, seem to depend on having words or symbols for those numbers.
1:25:551 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 55 SekundenWithout the words, you have the approximate sense, which is real and useful, but fundamentally imprecise.
1:26:041 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 4 SekundenHere's the thing that ties this back to our bigger story. Number words aren't just labels for quantities you already
1:26:111 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 11 Sekundenunderstand perfectly. They're cognitive tools that enable a kind of thinking you literally cannot do without them. They
1:26:191 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 19 Sekundencreate discrete categories out of a continuous approximate sense. They let you hold a precise quantity in working
1:26:261 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 26 Sekundenmemory, manipulate it, combine it with other quantities.
1:26:321 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 32 SekundenThe language doesn't just describe numerical thought. It makes certain numerical thought possible. And if
1:26:391 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 39 Sekundenthat's true for number, and the evidence is strong that it is, then it's probably true to varying degrees for other cognitive domains as well.
1:26:501 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 50 SekundenSpatial reasoning, temporal reasoning, social cognition, emotional granularity.
1:26:581 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 58 SekundenIn each of these domains, we've seen evidence that the linguistic tools available to you shape the cognitive operations you can perform. Not
1:27:071 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 7 Sekundenabsolutely, not as a prison, but as infrastructure, as scaffolding, as the difference
1:27:141 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 14 Sekundenbetween having a calculator and doing math in your head with your fingers.
Kapitel 16: Neural basis of language
1:27:201 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 20 SekundenWhich brings us to something that's been lurking in the background of this whole conversation.
1:27:251 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 25 Sekundensomething that the modern research has been slowly carefully building toward and that's the question of what's
1:27:321 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 32 Sekundenhappening in the brain. Because for all the behavioral evidence we've been talking about, reaction times, memory
1:27:411 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 41 Sekundenaccuracy, matching tasks, the real question is whether language is actually changing neural processing or
1:27:501 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 50 Sekundenwhether it's just influencing how people respond after they've already done their thinking. Is language in the perception or is it just in the report?
1:28:021 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 2 SekundenAnd starting in the mid 2000s, neuroscience began offering some genuinely surprising answers.
1:28:101 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 10 SekundenThere's a study that came out in 2008, and the setup was deceptively simple,
1:28:171 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 17 Sekundenbut what they found in the brain was genuinely startling.
1:28:221 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe researchers, and this was work led by Guom Tari and his colleagues at Bangor University in Wales, wanted to
1:28:301 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 30 Sekundenknow whether language categories affect color perception at a level that happens before conscious awareness, not after
1:28:381 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 38 Sekundenyou've already seen the color and thought about it. Before that, at the level of raw visual processing in the
1:28:471 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 47 Sekundenbrain. So, here's what they did. They showed people colored shapes on a screen and they used a technique called event
1:28:551 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 55 Sekundenrelated potentials or ERPS which is basically a way of measuring the brain's electrical response to a
1:29:031 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 3 Sekundenstimulus with very precise timing. We're talking milliseconds here. And the
1:29:101 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 10 Sekundenbeauty of ERPs is that different components of the brain's electrical response happen at different times after
1:29:171 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 17 Sekundena stimulus appears. And neuroscientists have gotten pretty good at figuring out what each of those components reflects.
1:29:261 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 26 SekundenEarly components, things happening in the first 100 to 200 milliseconds, tend to reflect perceptual processing,
1:29:351 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 35 Sekundenstuff happening before you've had time to think about what you're seeing.
1:29:391 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 39 SekundenLater, components reflect more deliberate conscious evaluation.
1:29:451 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 45 SekundenSo if language is only affecting your conscious postperceptual response, your report, your decision, your verbal
1:29:531 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 53 Sekundenlabeling, you'd expect to see effects only in those later components. The early perceptual stuff should be the
1:30:011 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 1 Sekundesame regardless of what language you speak. But that's not what they found.
1:30:071 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 7 SekundenTiari and his team tested Greek English bilingual and English monolingual.
1:30:131 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 13 SekundenAnd here's why Greek matters for this particular experiment. Greek, like Russian, remember Winau and Boroditsky's
1:30:211 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 21 Sekundenwork from earlier, makes a mandatory distinction between light blue and dark blue. In Greek, you have galazio for
1:30:291 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 29 Sekundenlight blue and blee for dark blue. And these aren't just optional descriptors.
1:30:351 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 35 SekundenThey're basic color terms. the way blue and green are basic and distinct categories in English. So Greek speakers
1:30:441 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 44 Sekundenin their everyday language are constantly distinguishing between these two blues as if they were as different
1:30:511 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 51 Sekundenas blue and green are to an English speaker.
1:30:551 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 55 SekundenWhat the researchers found was that Greek speakers showed a different brain response to light blue versus dark blue stimuli.
1:31:041 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 4 SekundenAnd here's the kicker. This difference showed up in an early visual processing component around 100 to 200 milliseconds
1:31:131 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 13 Sekundenafter the stimulus appeared. That's fast. That's before you've had time to consciously think about what you're
1:31:211 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 21 Sekundenseeing. Before you've had time to reach for a word, before any kind of deliberate categorization could kick in.
1:31:311 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 31 SekundenEnglish speakers who have one word for the whole blue range didn't show this early distinction.
1:31:381 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 38 SekundenThink about what that means. It suggests that years of speaking a language that carves the blue spectrum into two named
1:31:451 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 45 Sekundencategories had actually reorganized early visual processing in the brain.
1:31:511 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 51 SekundenThe language wasn't just affecting what people said about the colors or how they sorted them in a matching task. It was
1:31:581 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 58 Sekundenaffecting how their brains registered the colors in the first place.
1:32:031 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 3 SekundenThe linguistic boundary had become in some sense a perceptual boundary. Now
1:32:101 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 10 Sekundenyou might be thinking, okay, but maybe Greek speakers are just different for some other reason. Maybe it's cultural.
1:32:181 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 18 SekundenMaybe it's about how much time they spend looking at the Aian Sea. Fair question.
1:32:251 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 25 SekundenBut here's what makes the bilingual design so clever. When the researchers looked at the Greek English bilingual
1:32:331 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 33 Sekundenmore carefully, they found that the strength of this early perceptual effect correlated with how dominant the
1:32:401 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 40 Sekundenparticipants were in Greek. The more Greek dominant they were, the stronger the early brain response to the light
1:32:481 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 48 Sekundendark blue boundary. That's a dose response relationship and it points pretty strongly to language as the
1:32:551 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 55 Sekundendriving factor. And this wasn't an isolated finding. Around the same time, other labs were converging on similar
1:33:031 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 3 Sekundenresults using different methods. There's fascinating work by Paul K and Terry Regier. And remember, Kay was one of the
1:33:121 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 12 Sekundenresearchers who'd helped establish universal tendencies in color naming back in the '90s. So he wasn't someone predisposed to finding strong warian effects.
1:33:231 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 23 SekundenKay and colleagues along with Aubrey Gilbert found something really elegant.
1:33:301 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 30 SekundenThey showed that color categories from language affect perception more strongly in the right visual field than in the
1:33:381 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 38 Sekundenleft visual field. And here's why that matters. The right visual field is processed initially by the left
1:33:471 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 47 Sekundenhemisphere of the brain which is where language processing is predominantly located in most people. The left visual
1:33:551 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 55 Sekundenfield goes to the right hemisphere first. So if language is genuinely influencing perception, you'd expect the
1:34:031 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 3 Sekundeneffect to be stronger for stimuli that land in the language dominant hemisphere first. And that's exactly what they found.
1:34:131 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 13 SekundenWhen colors straddled a linguistic boundary, say one was blue and one was green, people were faster at detecting
1:34:211 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 21 Sekundenthe difference when the colors appeared in the right visual field, feeding into the left language dominant hemisphere.
1:34:291 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 29 SekundenWhen the colors appeared in the left visual field, the advantage shrank or disappeared.
1:34:361 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 36 SekundenThat's a really specific anatomically grounded prediction. And the fact that it holds up is powerful evidence that
1:34:441 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 44 Sekundenlanguage isn't just sitting on top of perception like a label you slap on afterward. It's woven into the
1:34:511 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 51 Sekundenperceptual process itself. At least under certain conditions, the language is in the seeing. But and this is
1:35:001 Stunde, 35 Minutenimportant. It's not there all the time and it's not there in the same way for everything.
1:35:081 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 8 SekundenAnd this is where the neuroscience actually helps us refine the picture rather than just confirming a simple language controls perception story.
1:35:191 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 19 SekundenThere's beautiful work by Gary Lupian who's been one of the most creative researchers in this space.
1:35:261 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 26 SekundenLupian has shown that language effects on perception are what you might call on demand. They can be turned on and off.
1:35:351 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 35 SekundenIn one clever experiment, he had people do a visual search task. Find the odd one out in an array of shapes. When he
1:35:441 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 44 Sekundengave them a verbal label before the search, look for the two among a bunch of fives, for instance, their visual
1:35:521 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 52 Sekundenprocessing was measurably enhanced. The label sharpened their perception of the target.
1:35:591 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 59 SekundenBut when the label was absent or when it was replaced by a nonverbal cue, the enhancement went away.
1:36:071 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 7 SekundenLanguage was acting like a top- down filter, tuning the visual system to be more sensitive to the features that the word picks out.
1:36:151 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 15 SekundenAnd here's what's really clever about Lupian's framework. He argues that language doesn't just passively reflect categories that exist in the world.
1:36:251 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 25 Sekundenit warps perception toward those categories.
1:36:301 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 30 SekundenHe calls this the label feedback hypothesis.
1:36:341 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 34 SekundenThe idea is that when you activate a word, even unconsciously, even just because you're a speaker of a language
1:36:411 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 41 Sekundenthat has that word, it sends feedback signals from language areas down into perceptual areas, biasing what you see toward the prototype of that category.
1:36:521 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 52 SekundenSo when a Russian speaker sees a shade of blue that's near the boundary between Galazio and Sy, sorry I'm mixing up my
1:37:001 Stunde, 37 Minutenlanguages, between Golib boy and sin, the light and dark blue terms in Russian. The activation of the relevant
1:37:091 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 9 Sekundenword nudges perception toward one prototype or the other, making the color look a little more light blue or a
1:37:171 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 17 Sekundenlittle more dark blue than it might to someone whose language doesn't make that cut. It's like having a slight
1:37:241 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 24 Sekundengravitational pull around each named category. Colors near the boundary get tugged one way or the other. And this
1:37:331 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 33 Sekundenhappens fast and it happens automatically. But it can also be disrupted.
1:37:391 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 39 SekundenThis is the part that keeps the story honest.
1:37:431 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 43 SekundenSeveral studies have shown that if you give people a verbal interference task, have them repeat a word over and over
1:37:501 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 50 Sekundenwhile they're doing a color discrimination task, which essentially ties up their language system, the cross-linguistic differences in color perception shrink or disappear.
1:38:021 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 2 Sekundentake language offline and the effect goes away.
1:38:071 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 7 SekundenWhich means the effect is genuinely linguistic, not just some deep cultural imprint that has nothing to do with the words themselves.
1:38:161 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 16 SekundenThe words are doing actual work in real time.
1:38:201 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 20 SekundenSo now we have this picture that's really quite nuanced.
1:38:251 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 25 SekundenLanguage affects perception, but it does so through an active online process where linguistic categories send
1:38:331 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 33 Sekundenfeedback into perceptual systems. It's not that speaking Greek literally rewires your retina. Your retina works
1:38:421 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 42 Sekundenthe same way regardless of what language you speak. But somewhere between the retina and conscious experience,
1:38:491 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 49 Sekundenlanguage is modulating the signal. It's amplifying differences that cross linguistic boundaries and compressing
1:38:561 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 56 Sekundendifferences that fall within a single category. And it's doing this quickly,
1:39:031 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 3 Sekundenautomatically, and as the ERP data shows, at a stage of processing that precedes conscious deliberation.
1:39:121 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 12 SekundenAnd this framework, language as an active modulator of cognition, not a passive container for pre-existing
1:39:191 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 19 Sekundenthoughts, has implications that go way beyond color. Let's think about spatial cognition for a moment. Remember the Kuk
1:39:281 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 28 Sekundenthyora, the Aboriginal community that Boraditzky studied, the ones who use absolute spatial coordinates instead of
1:39:361 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 36 Sekundenrelative ones, who say north and south instead of left and right.
1:39:431 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 43 SekundenWell, there's been neuroiming work suggesting that people who habitually use absolute spatial frames show different patterns of activation in
1:39:521 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 52 Sekundenspatial processing areas of the brain compared to people who use relative frames.
1:39:581 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 58 SekundenThe constant practice of tracking cardinal directions, which is demanded by the language because you can't form a grammatical sentence without knowing
1:40:071 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 7 Sekundenwhich way is north, appears to tune up the neural systems involved in spatial orientation. It's like the difference
1:40:141 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 14 Sekundenbetween someone who plays piano every day and someone who doesn't. The pianist's motor cortex looks different.
1:40:221 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe neural pathways involved in fine motor control of the fingers are thicker, more elaborated, more
1:40:281 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 28 Sekundenefficient. Not because the pianist was born with different hardware, but because years of practice sculpted the hardware into a different configuration.
1:40:391 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 39 Sekundenlanguage seems to do something similar for cognitive systems. If your language requires you to constantly track cardinal directions, your spatial
1:40:481 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 48 Sekundenprocessing networks get trained up. If your language requires you to distinguish light blue from dark blue every time you open your mouth, your
1:40:571 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 57 Sekundencolor processing gets tuned to that boundary. And this connects to something that Larara Boreditzky has emphasized in her more recent synthetic work. She's
1:41:061 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 6 Sekundenargued that language should be thought of as a form of cognitive training.
1:41:111 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 11 SekundenEvery time you speak, every time you listen, every time you formulate a thought in words, you're running your
1:41:181 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 18 Sekundencognitive systems through a particular set of exercises. And just like physical exercise shapes the body, cognitive
1:41:261 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 26 Sekundenlinguistic exercise shapes the mind. Not by constraining it. You can always learn new exercises, new languages, new ways
1:41:351 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 35 Sekundenof thinking, but by making certain patterns more practiced, more fluent, more automatic than others. This is a
1:41:441 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 44 Sekundenlong way from Benjamin Lee Horf's original formulation, and in some ways it's more interesting. Orf imagined
1:41:511 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 51 Sekundenlanguage as a kind of lens that you were stuck behind, unable to see the world except through its particular distortions.
1:41:591 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 59 SekundenThe modern picture is more like language as a set of well-worn paths through a forest. You can always bushwack off the
1:42:061 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 6 Sekundentrail. You can always learn to see the forest differently, but the paths are there and they're easier to walk. And
1:42:161 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 16 Sekundenmost of the time, without even thinking about it, you follow them. And over years and decades of following those
1:42:241 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 24 Sekundenpaths, they get wider and smoother, and the forest around them gets a little less familiar.
Kapitel 17: The cost of language loss
1:42:311 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 31 SekundenWhich brings us to a question that's been gaining urgency in recent years.
1:42:361 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 36 SekundenAnd it's not just an academic question anymore. It's a question with real consequences for education, for policy,
1:42:441 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 44 Sekundenfor technology, for how we think about artificial intelligence and the future of human communication.
1:42:511 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 51 SekundenAnd that question is if language shapes thought in all these ways we've been talking about through color perception,
1:43:001 Stunde, 43 Minutenspatial reasoning, numerical cognition, emotional granularity, moral judgment, memory for events. Then what happens when languages die?
1:43:111 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 11 SekundenBecause languages are dying, they're dying at an extraordinary rate, linguists estimate that of the roughly
1:43:181 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 18 Sekunden7,000 languages spoken on Earth today, somewhere between a third and a half will fall silent by the end of this
1:43:251 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 25 Sekundencentury. That's a language going extinct roughly every 2 weeks. And each one of those languages represents not just a
1:43:341 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 34 Sekundencommunication system, not just a cultural artifact, but if the research we've been discussing is right, a unique
1:43:421 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 42 Sekundencognitive toolkit, a particular way of carving up reality, of attending to certain features of experience and backgrounding others, of
1:43:521 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 52 Sekundentraining the mind along particular paths.
1:43:551 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 55 SekundenWhen a language dies, we don't just lose words. We lose the cognitive habits that those words sustain.
1:44:041 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 4 SekundenWe lose the perceptual distinctions they sharpen, the conceptual categories they maintain, the reasoning patterns they
1:44:121 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 12 Sekundenencourage. We lose ways of thinking that no one will ever think again. And unlike a building or a painting, you can't put
1:44:211 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 21 Sekundena dead language in a museum and preserve what it did to the minds of its speakers. The cognitive effects are
1:44:281 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 28 Sekundenalive only as long as the language is alive, being spoken, being heard, being used to think with.
1:44:381 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 38 SekundenAnd this isn't just a poetic lament.
1:44:411 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 41 SekundenThere are researchers who are trying to document not just the grammars and vocabularies of endangered languages but their cognitive consequences.
1:44:511 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 51 SekundenWhat spatial reasoning strategies do speakers of this language use? What temporal metaphors structure their thinking?
1:45:001 Stunde, 45 MinutenWhat emotional distinctions does their lexicon support?
1:45:041 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 4 SekundenBecause once the last speaker is gone, those questions become unanswerable. The experiment can never be run again.
1:45:131 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 13 SekundenThere's a researcher named Nicholas Evans, a linguist at the Australian National University, who's been particularly eloquent about this.
1:45:231 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 23 SekundenEvans has argued that linguistic diversity is a kind of natural experiment in human cognition.
1:45:301 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 30 SekundenThousands of different languages, each one a slightly different configuration of the cognitive toolkit. Each one
1:45:371 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 37 Sekundenrevealing something about the range of what human minds can do. And we're watching that experiment
1:45:441 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 44 Sekundenbe dismantled language by language, community by community, generation by generation.
1:45:531 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 53 SekundenBut there's another side to this story, and it has to do with where language is going, not just where it's disappearing.
1:46:011 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 1 SekundeBecause at the same time that thousands of small languages are fading, a few global languages are expanding their
1:46:081 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 8 Sekundenreach and entirely new forms of language. emoji code, the compressed
1:46:151 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 15 Sekundenshorthand of social media, the prompt languages people use to communicate with AI systems are emerging and evolving at
1:46:241 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 24 Sekundena pace that would have astonished linguists a generation ago. And if language shapes thought, then these new
1:46:321 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 32 Sekundenlinguistic forms must be shaping thought, too, in ways we're only beginning to understand.
1:46:391 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 39 SekundenAnd that's a genuinely fascinating thought to sit with for a moment because we tend to think of language change as
Kapitel 18: Future of language and thought
1:46:461 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 46 Sekundensomething that happens slowly over centuries. The way Latin gradually became French and Spanish and Italian.
1:46:551 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 55 SekundenBut what's happening right now is different. The pace is extraordinary.
1:47:001 Stunde, 47 MinutenNew words, new grammars, new entire registers of communication are emerging in years, sometimes months, and billions
1:47:101 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 10 Sekundenof people are adopting them almost simultaneously.
1:47:151 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 15 SekundenAnd if everything we've been talking about tonight is true, if the language you use really does shape the way you
1:47:221 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 22 Sekundenperceive color, remember events, navigate space, feel emotions, make moral judgments,
1:47:321 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 32 Sekundenthen we are right now in the middle of one of the largest uncontrolled cognitive experiments in human history.
1:47:401 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 40 SekundenAnd almost nobody is talking about it in those terms. Let's start with something simple.
1:47:471 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 47 SekundenEmoji.
1:47:491 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 49 SekundenYou might think of emoji as trivial, as little cartoon faces people stick at the end of text messages, but think about what they actually do.
1:47:581 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 58 SekundenThey're a visual vocabulary layered on top of written language, and they've become remarkably standardized across
1:48:061 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 6 Sekundencultures. There are over 3,000 emoji in the Unicode standard as of the early 2020s. And they're used by billions of
1:48:151 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 15 Sekundenpeople every day. And here's what's interesting. From a Horian perspective, emoji create categorical distinctions
1:48:231 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 23 Sekundenthat spoken language doesn't always make. Think about the difference between a slight smile, a grinning face, a face with tears of joy, and a smirking face.
1:48:351 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 35 SekundenIn spoken English, you might use the word happy or amused to cover all of those. But emoji users learn to
1:48:441 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 44 Sekundendistinguish between these emotional states with precision. Selecting exactly the right face for exactly the right shade of feeling. That's emotional
1:48:531 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 53 Sekundengranularity. The same concept Lisa Feldman Barrett studies. And it's being trained not by a rich spoken vocabulary
1:49:021 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 2 Sekundenof emotion words, but by a set of tiny yellow faces on a screen.
1:49:071 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 7 SekundenThere's early research and I want to be honest this is genuinely early stage work not the kind of well-replicated
1:49:161 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 16 Sekundenfindings we talked about with Wawa or Boraditzky but there's early research suggesting that heavy emoji users do
1:49:241 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 24 Sekundendevelop finer grained emotional categorization in digital contexts they become better at reading emotional tone
1:49:331 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 33 Sekundenin text messages they develop a kind of literacy for digital emotional expression that non-users don't share.
1:49:411 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 41 SekundenWhether that transfers to face-to-face emotional perception is an open question, but the direction of the
1:49:491 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 49 Sekundenevidence is suggestive, and it maps beautifully onto the framework we've been building all night. Language is a
1:49:561 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 56 Sekundengroove, not a prison. Emoji aren't replacing spoken emotional vocabulary.
1:50:031 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 3 SekundenThey're adding another layer, another set of well-worn paths through the landscape of emotional experience.
1:50:111 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 11 SekundenNow, think about something more consequential.
1:50:141 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 14 SekundenThink about code, computer programming languages.
1:50:201 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 20 SekundenThere are millions of people in the world and the number is growing rapidly for whom a significant portion of their
1:50:261 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 26 Sekundendaily thinking is done in Python or JavaScript or C++ or SQL.
1:50:351 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 35 SekundenAnd these are languages in a very real sense. They have syntax, they have grammar, they have vocabulary, they
1:50:431 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 43 Sekundenenforce particular ways of structuring problems. And here's the thing, different programming languages enforce genuinely different cognitive styles.
1:50:551 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 55 SekundenIf you think in Python, you tend to think in terms of objects and their properties, clean readable sequences of instructions, elegant abstractions.
1:51:051 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 5 SekundenIf you think in C, you tend to think closer to the machine, memory allocation, pointers, the physical architecture of the hardware.
1:51:151 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 15 SekundenIf you think in SQL, you think in terms of sets, relationships between tables of data, queries that filter and combine.
1:51:251 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 25 SekundenFunctional programming languages like Haskell encourage you to think about computation as the composition of
1:51:321 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 32 Sekundenmathematical functions, avoiding the concept of changing state entirely.
1:51:381 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 38 SekundenProgrammers know this intuitively. They talk about it all the time.
1:51:441 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 44 SekundenLearning a new programming language doesn't just give you a new tool. It gives you a new way of thinking about
1:51:511 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 51 Sekundenproblems. There's a famous quote attributed to Alan Pearles, one of the pioneers of computer science, who said
1:52:001 Stunde, 52 Minutenthat a language that doesn't affect the way you think about programming is not worth knowing. And that's pure
1:52:071 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 7 Sekundensapperworf applied to artificial languages.
1:52:121 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 12 SekundenThe structure of the language shapes the structure of thought. And unlike natural languages where the effects can be
1:52:201 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 20 Sekundensubtle and hard to isolate, with programming languages, the effects are often dramatic and obvious. A problem
1:52:281 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 28 Sekundenthat's elegant and natural to express in one language can be awkward and convoluted in another.
1:52:361 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 36 SekundenThe language doesn't just describe the solution.
1:52:391 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 39 SekundenIt shapes what solutions you can even imagine. There's a deeper point here, too.
1:52:461 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 46 SekundenProgramming languages are the first languages in human history that were consciously designed with specific cognitive goals in mind.
1:52:551 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 55 SekundenNatural languages evolved. Nobody sat down and decided that English would have tenses or that Mandarin would use tone.
1:53:031 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 3 SekundenBut programming languages are engineered. Their designers make deliberate choices about what concepts to make easy to express and what
1:53:121 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 12 Sekundenconcepts to make difficult or impossible.
1:53:161 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 16 SekundenAnd those choices have cognitive consequences for millions of users.
1:53:221 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 22 SekundenIn a sense, programming language designers are doing what imagined language doing, shaping the thought
1:53:301 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 30 Sekundenpatterns of their users, but they're doing it on purpose. And then there's the compressed language of social media.
1:53:381 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 38 SekundenTwitter, now X, famously imposed 140 character limit, later expanded to 280.
1:53:471 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 47 SekundenTik Tok videos are measured in seconds.
1:53:501 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 50 SekundenInstagram captions compete with images for attention. These platforms don't just distribute language, they shape it.
1:53:581 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 58 SekundenThey select for brevity, for punch, for emotional immediiacy.
1:54:041 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 4 SekundenComplex arguments get compressed into slogans. Nuanced positions get flattened into takes. And the question from a
1:54:121 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 12 Sekundenlanguage and thought perspective is, does habitual use of these compressed forms actually change the way people
1:54:191 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 19 Sekundenthink? Does spending years communicating in short bursts affect your capacity for sustained complex reasoning?
1:54:291 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 29 SekundenThe honest answer is we don't know yet.
1:54:331 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe research is too young and the confounding variables are enormous. But there are suggestive findings.
1:54:411 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 41 SekundenThere's work by researchers like Gloria Mark at the University of California, Irvine, who's documented that average
1:54:481 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 48 Sekundenattention spans on screens have been shrinking from about 2 1/2 minutes on a given task in the early 2000s to about 47 seconds by the mid2010s.
1:54:591 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 59 SekundenNow, attention span isn't the same as linguistic cognition, but they're related.
1:55:061 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 6 SekundenIf your habitual mode of communication is the quick reply, the short post, the rapid fire exchange, it's at least
1:55:141 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 14 Sekundenplausible that this is training certain cognitive grooves at the expense of others. You're getting very good at
1:55:221 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 22 Sekundenquick emotional assessment, at rapid social signaling, at compressing meaning into minimal space.
1:55:291 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 29 Sekundenand you might be getting less practiced at the kind of slow, recursive, deeply nested reasoning that long form language
1:55:371 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 37 Sekundensupports. This connects back to something we touched on much earlier in the evening when we talked about how language isn't just a tool for communication.
1:55:471 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 47 SekundenIt's a tool for thinking. And the kind of thinking you can do depends at least in part on the kind of linguistic tools
1:55:551 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 55 Sekundenyou have available and the kind you habitually use.
1:55:591 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 59 SekundenIf your daily linguistic practice is dominated by short form digital communication, that's the cognitive groove that gets deepened. And the
1:56:071 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 7 Sekundengrooves for other kinds of thinking, the long, patient, carefully qualified kind, might gradually become less worn, harder
1:56:161 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 16 Sekundento find your way back to. But here's where I want to pull back from anything that sounds like doom and gloom. Because
1:56:241 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 24 Sekundenthe story is more complicated than that and more interesting. Because these new forms of language aren't just impoverished versions of older forms.
1:56:341 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 34 SekundenThey're genuinely new and they support cognitive operations that older forms didn't. Think about hashtags.
1:56:431 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 43 SekundenA hashtag is a remarkable linguistic invention.
1:56:481 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 48 SekundenIt's a word or phrase that simultaneously communicates meaning and categorizes itself, linking the utterance to every other utterance
1:56:561 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 56 Sekundenthat's ever used the same tag. That's a kind of intertextual connection that has no real precedent in spoken language.
1:57:051 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 5 SekundenWhen you write a hashtag, you're not just saying something. You're placing your statement in a vast web of related
1:57:121 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 12 Sekundenstatements and you know it and your reader knows it. That's a new cognitive move. It's a new way of thinking about
1:57:201 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 20 Sekundenhow your words relate to the words of others. Or think about the way people communicate with AI systems. Now, prompt
1:57:291 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 29 Sekundenengineering, the art of crafting inputs to large language models to get useful outputs is essentially a new linguistic
1:57:371 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 37 Sekundenskill and it requires a fascinating kind of metacognitive awareness.
1:57:431 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 43 SekundenYou have to think about how the AI will interpret your words, which means you have to think about your own language from the outside, modeling how a
1:57:531 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 53 Sekundennonhuman intelligence will parse your meaning. That's a kind of linguistic self-consciousness that's genuinely
1:58:001 Stunde, 58 Minutennovel. It's almost the opposite of normal language use where you're trying to be understood by someone who shares
1:58:071 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 7 Sekundenyour cognitive framework. With AI, you're trying to be understood by something that processes language in a
1:58:141 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 14 Sekundenfundamentally different way. And that requires you to think about the structure of your own language more explicitly than you normally would. So where does all of this leave us?
1:58:261 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 26 SekundenAfter everything we've wandered through tonight, from Aristotle's conviction that language merely labels pre-existing thoughts, through Humbult's romantic
1:58:351 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 35 Sekundenvision of each language as a unique worldview, through Boas and Sappir and Horf and the strong hypothesis that got
1:58:431 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 43 Sekundenso thoroughly rejected, through Chsky's universalism, through the quiet experimental renaissance of the 1990s,
1:58:511 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 51 Sekundenthrough Russian blues and kuk thyora compass directions and bilingual moral reasoning and home signers and
1:58:581 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 58 SekundenNicaraguan sign language and ERP waves and right visual field effects and the label feedback hypothesis.
1:59:071 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 7 SekundenAfter all of that, what do we actually know?
1:59:121 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 12 SekundenHere's what I think we know. And I want to say this gently because the beauty of this question is that it's still open, still alive, still being investigated.
1:59:231 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 23 SekundenWe know that the strong Horian hypothesis that language determines thought that you literally cannot think
1:59:311 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 31 Sekundenwhat your language cannot express is almost certainly wrong. The evidence against it is too strong. People can
1:59:401 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 40 Sekundenthink without language. Babies think, animals think, home signers think.
1:59:471 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 47 SekundenSpeakers of languages that lack certain words or structures can still grasp the concepts when they need to. The prison
1:59:551 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 55 Sekundenmetaphor doesn't hold. But we also know, and this is supported by decades of careful experimental work now, that
2:00:042 Stunden, 4 Sekundenlanguage profoundly influences thought in ways that are real, measurable, and consequential.
2:00:102 Stunden, 10 SekundenIt shapes perception at very early stages of visual processing.
2:00:152 Stunden, 15 SekundenIt guides attention. It structures memory. It calibrates emotional experience. It biases moral reasoning.
2:00:242 Stunden, 24 SekundenIt provides the scaffolding for complex cognitive operations like exact number and recursive reasoning. It does all of
2:00:322 Stunden, 32 Sekundenthis not by imprisoning thought, but by making certain thoughts easier, more natural, more automatic.
2:00:422 Stunden, 42 SekundenThe groove metaphor holds up remarkably well. Language is a groove, a set of well-worn paths through the vast
2:00:512 Stunden, 51 Sekundenlandscape of possible thoughts. And while you can always step off the path, the paths you walk most often are the ones that feel like home.
2:01:022 Stunden, 1 Minute, 2 SekundenAnd here's what I find most beautiful about that conclusion.
2:01:072 Stunden, 1 Minute, 7 SekundenIt means that every language, every single one of the 7,000 or so languages spoken on Earth right now, and every one
2:01:152 Stunden, 1 Minute, 15 Sekundenof the countless languages that have ever been spoken and are now silent, represents a unique set of paths through the landscape of human cognition.
2:01:262 Stunden, 1 Minute, 26 Sekunden[clears throat] Each one highlights different features of reality, makes different distinctions automatic, supports different kinds of reasoning.
2:01:352 Stunden, 1 Minute, 35 SekundenAnd that means that the full range of human cognitive potential isn't captured by any single language. It's distributed
2:01:442 Stunden, 1 Minute, 44 Sekundenacross all of them. The totality of what human minds can do is written not in any
2:01:512 Stunden, 1 Minute, 51 Sekundenone grammar, but in the collective diversity of every grammar that has ever existed.
2:01:572 Stunden, 1 Minute, 57 SekundenAnd you lying there right now, you carry some of those paths in your own mind.
2:02:042 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 4 SekundenThe language you grew up speaking, the languages you learned later, the dialects and registers and digital
2:02:122 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 12 Sekundenshorthands you've absorbed, all of them have shaped the particular configuration of your cognitive landscape,
2:02:202 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 20 Sekundenthe way you see colors, the way you remember yesterday, the way you feel about the future, the way you navigate a
2:02:272 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 27 Sekundenmoral dilemma. All of it bears the quiet imprint of the words you've lived with.
2:02:332 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 33 SekundenNot as a cage, not as a prison, but as a home, a familiar place with well-worn
2:02:412 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 41 Sekundenpaths between the rooms. Paths you walk so often you've forgotten their paths at all.
2:02:492 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 49 SekundenAnd maybe that's the gentlest, truest thing that can be said about language and thought here at the end of a long
